        rules: {
          objective: 'Be the first player to reach 15 points (2-player) or 10 points (3–4 player).',
          gameplay: 'Players take turns extending the line of dominoes from either end. Scoring occurs when the end values match. If a player cannot move, they draw from the boneyard (leaving two tiles). The first to empty their hand scores a bonus.',
          keyRules: [
            'Doubles are played crosswise but are NOT spinners — the line extends only from the two ends, not from the sides of the double.',
            'Two tiles must always remain in the boneyard and cannot be drawn.',
            'Score immediately when a double header or triple header is formed.',
            'First player to reach target score (15 or 10) wins; the current hand is completed before checking the total.',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        strategy: {
          basic: 'Play tiles that create double headers or triple headers for immediate points. Prioritise playing doubles to set up a triple header.',
          intermediate: "Suit counting: each pip value appears on 8 tile-ends in a double-6 set. Once a double is played, half that suit's ends are taken up. Use this to predict whether a double header will hold or be broken. Hold doubles as long as possible to convert a double header into a triple header.",
          advanced: 'Blocking strategy: if you realise you cannot score easily, aim to block the game by managing which suits are available at the ends. A blocked game awards 2 points to the lightest hand — sometimes deliberately constrain the ends to force a block when you hold fewer tiles than opponents. Track what opponents have drawn (if any) to infer their tile composition.',
          tips: [
            'Doubles are the most powerful tiles — they are both required for triple headers and hard to play offensively by opponents.',
            'Target the same pip value at both ends as soon as possible; a triple header (3 pts) beats two separate double headers (4 pts total) only if you can sustain it.',
            'In a blocked game, the player with the fewest tiles scores 2 pts — factor this into late-game play when your hand is light.',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
